    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 130,
      "topLeftY": 478,
      "bottomRightX": 605,
      "bottomRightY": 565,
      "content": "If we next consider a *second* observation to be made, where our total state is now a superposition, we can apply Rule 1 separately to each element of the superposition, since each element separately obeys the wave equation and behaves independently of the remaining elements, and then superpose the results to obtain the final solution. We formulate this as:",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 164,
      "topLeftY": 574,
      "bottomRightX": 605,
      "bottomRightY": 647,
      "content": "*Rule 2:* Rule 1 may be applied separately to each element of a superposition of total system states, the results being superposed to obtain the final total state. Thus, a determination of $B$, with eigenfunctions $\\eta_j^{S_2}$, on $S_2$ by the observer $0$ transforms the total state",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
